WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.418
A proxy is an intermediary: it accepts the
client's request, knows which upstream service

00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:11.019
can answer it, and relays the response. Add
storage to that intermediary and it can remember

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:16.620
reusable answers. On the first request for
article A, the cache has no matching entry, so
it

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.055
forwards the request to the application.

00:00:19.055 --> 00:00:24.141
The application reads data and constructs the
response; the proxy saves an eligible copy

00:00:24.141 --> 00:00:29.401
while returning it. A second equivalent request
can be answered directly from that copy. In

00:00:29.401 --> 00:00:33.621
this example, notice the miss visiting origin
and the hit stopping early.

00:00:33.621 --> 00:00:38.447
The performance gain comes from avoiding
repeated work, not from making the original

00:00:38.447 --> 00:00:40.343
computation intrinsically faster.

00:00:40.343 --> 00:00:46.318
Let us actually run the eviction policy. There
is room for three equally sized items. After

00:00:46.318 --> 00:00:52.359
accessing A, then B, then C, the shelf reads A,
B, C from oldest to newest. All three spaces

00:00:52.359 --> 00:00:58.400
are occupied. The question is not simply which
objects are present. We also need to remember

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.319
their recency.

00:00:59.343 --> 00:01:05.077
Now access A again. This is a hit, so we do not
fetch another copy. But we do change the

00:01:05.077 --> 00:01:10.550
order. Move A to the newest end. The shelf now
reads B, C, A. B has become the least

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:14.524
recently used item. A hit changes the next
eviction decision.

00:01:14.524 --> 00:01:20.548
Finally, D arrives. The shelf is full, so remove
B and place D at the newest end. The result

00:01:20.548 --> 00:01:26.310
is C, A, D. LRU uses recent access as a
prediction of future usefulness. That prediction

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:31.876
often helps with temporal locality, but a scan
of unrelated objects can still evict a

00:01:31.876 --> 00:01:33.251
valuable working set.

00:01:33.251 --> 00:01:38.160
Why can a small cache remove a large fraction of
service traffic? Requests are often

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.478
unevenly distributed. A few popular articles
receive repeated reads while many less popular

00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:48.737
articles form a long tail. If a cache stores the
frequently requested subset, its fraction

00:01:48.737 --> 00:01:54.055
of all stored documents can be small even while
its fraction of answered requests is large.

00:01:54.251 --> 00:01:59.328
For an illustrative workload of one thousand
requests per second with a ninety percent hit

00:01:59.328 --> 00:02:04.461
ratio, only about one hundred misses per second
reach origin, ignoring validation and other

00:02:04.461 --> 00:02:09.369
traffic. This example separates the hot set from
the long tail rather than treating all

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:11.851
documents as equally likely to be requested.

00:02:11.851 --> 00:02:17.073
The price of reuse appears when an authoritative
value changes after a cached copy was

00:02:17.073 --> 00:02:22.598
saved. Suppose a public timetable response can
remain fresh for sixty seconds. At time zero

00:02:22.598 --> 00:02:27.335
the proxy stores version one; at time twenty the
origin publishes version two.

00:02:27.351 --> 00:02:32.413
An otherwise eligible request at time forty can
still receive version one under that

00:02:32.413 --> 00:02:37.897
freshness policy. At expiry, the cache must
refresh or validate before ordinary reuse. This

00:02:37.897 --> 00:02:43.260
example places the edit and expiry on separate
paths to show why updating origin does not

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.947
instantly update every copy.

00:02:44.947 --> 00:02:49.971
Select a freshness window from the application's
tolerance for stale information, not only

00:02:49.971 --> 00:02:52.148
from the hit ratio you hope to achieve.
